      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "We can integrate over \\(y\\) rather than \\(x\\). In particular, the solution is \\(\\int_{0}^{\\pi / 4} \\sin y \\mathrm{~d} y+\\) \\(\\int_{\\pi / 4}^{\\pi / 2} \\cos y \\mathrm{~d} y=\\left(1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right) 2=2-\\sqrt{2}\\).",
